On Mon, 4 Aug 2014, Andres wrote:
> Or we could just ignore him.
> Eventually he'll get tired and go away and there aren't many more reports
> of griefers out there. Why potentially punish innocent players for a
> temporary problem?

There's no potential for punishing innocents.

Ray's scheme would involve manual identification of griefers, presumably
by examining ttyrec's of users who are complained about.

My scheme could false-positive, but the only consequence would be no 
bones dropped on the particular suspect game.

The reason the topic is worthy of discussion is that people should know
that technical solutions are possible.  A public server doesn't have to
put up with griefing if it doesn't want to, although of course the
decision belongs to their admins, not the players.


However, if *I* were arguing for inaction, I'd cite the DevTeam's past
behavior as indicating unfair bones are part of the legitimate game.

First, consider what happens when someone on the way up with The Amulet
dies on level 4.  The resulting bones, if dropped, would be very unfair to
a starting player, since high-level monsters may be wandering the level.

The DevTeam obviously knows this can happen, since they put in special
code to handle the case.  But that code merely despawns the Wizard (if
present) and replaces the Amulet with a fake.  It doesn't stop the bones
from being dropped.

Bones on the minetown level can also be nasty, since the Temple can be
ruined, denying a chance to buy protection at low level.  Minetown could
have been flagged no-bones, but isn't.

